# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored; this template is not.
# NEVER put real values here — this file is committed, and the repo is public.

# ── Supabase ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Dashboard > Project Settings > API. Project region is ca-central-1 (Montreal),
# paired with Vercel functions in iad1 (see vercel.json).
#
# Both of these are PUBLIC by design: they ship in the browser bundle. They
# identify the project; they authorise nothing on their own. Every permission
# decision is made by RLS against the caller's JWT.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_<...>

# The SERVICE ROLE key bypasses RLS completely, so a single leak exposes every
# facility's data. It is listed here — rather than left undocumented — because
# ONE route genuinely needs it and a key people add ad-hoc without knowing what
# it does is worse than one written down with the warning attached.
#
# Used by exactly one thing: creating the auth account for a new hire
# (src/app/api/staff/[id]/invite/route.ts, via src/lib/supabase/admin.ts, which
# is `server-only` so importing it into client code fails the build). Creating a
# user is GoTrue's admin API; RLS cannot do it. Every other statement in that
# route goes through the caller's own client so RLS still decides.
#
# NEVER prefix it NEXT_PUBLIC_ — that publishes it to every visitor. Unset, the
# invite route returns `sent:false, reason:"not_configured"` and sends nothing,
# which is the honest answer rather than a half-created hire.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ── WorkOS AuthKit (the identity provider — ADR 0004) ───────────────────────
# Clerk held this slot until 2026-08-17 and is gone: no packages, no variables,
# no registration in Supabase. If you are reading a `CLERK_*` name anywhere, it
# is stale — see ADR 0004 for why the provider changed (per-MAU cost at the
# scale this product is sold at).
#
# Client ID and API key come from the WorkOS dashboard, PER ENVIRONMENT
# (Staging and Production are separate apps with separate keys). The client ID
# also forms the Supabase third-party auth issuer:
#   https://api.workos.com/user_management/<WORKOS_CLIENT_ID>
WORKOS_CLIENT_ID=client_<...>
WORKOS_API_KEY=sk_test_<...>

# 32+ characters. This encrypts the sealed session cookie, so changing it signs
# everybody out. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
WORKOS_COOKIE_PASSWORD=

# Must EQUAL the callback route (src/app/auth/callback/route.ts) and be
# registered as a redirect URI on the WorkOS environment, or the provider
# refuses the hand-off with `Invalid redirect_uri`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_WORKOS_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/auth/callback

# Signing secret for the user-sync webhook, PER ENDPOINT — so Staging and
# Production have DIFFERENT values, and the wrong one is not a degraded state:
# every delivery fails signature verification with a 400.
#
# The dashboard shows it once, but the API returns it on creation, so the whole
# thing is one command (the key decides the environment):
#
#   workos webhook create --api-key sk_test_… \
#     --url https://<host>/api/webhooks/workos \
#     --events user.created,user.updated,user.deleted --json
#
# Read by src/app/api/webhooks/workos/route.ts. Unset, every delivery is
# rejected 500 and no new user ever gets a profiles row — which reads as
# "sign-in works but every page is empty".
#
# That route ALSO needs SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above: a webhook carries no
# session, so an RLS-bound client would be `anon` and every write refused.
#
# WorkOS cannot reach http://localhost, so local sign-ups produce no profiles
# row unless you tunnel (and point a staging endpoint at the tunnel URL):
#
#   cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:3000
#
# A quick tunnel's hostname changes every restart. UPDATE the endpoint's URL
# rather than creating a second one — the secret belongs to the endpoint, so
# remaking it means re-copying a secret for no reason. Diagnose with a forged
# signature: 400 means reachable and configured, 500 means the secret (or the
# service-role key) is missing.
WORKOS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Cookie domain — LEAVE UNSET for localhost. Facilities get their own hosts
# (pawradise.yipyy.com, spec 002 D2) and the session cookie must be readable on
# all of them, so production sets `.yipyy.com` WITH THE LEADING DOT. Locally,
# set `.yipyy.test` only when testing facility hosts, and then browse
# yipyy.test:3000 rather than localhost:3000.
#
# A wrong value fails SILENTLY: sign-in appears to succeed and bounces straight
# back to the login page.
# WORKOS_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.yipyy.com

# ── AI ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ── Email (Resend) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RESEND_API_KEY=
EMAIL_FROM=

# ── Telephony (Twilio) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# YIPYY's OWN account — the PARENT. Not any facility's.
#
# Each facility gets a Twilio SUBACCOUNT created over the API from these
# credentials, with its own SID, its own auth token and its own numbers. Those
# per-facility tokens go into Supabase Vault (private.communication_credentials)
# and are reached through public.communication_auth_token — never here, and
# never in the browser.
#
# So this pair is the keys to every facility's line at once, which is exactly
# why it is an environment variable rather than a row: there is no facility it
# belongs to, and any table that could hold it is a table somebody can be given
# a policy on by mistake.
#
# There WAS a client-side store, src/hooks/use-twilio-config.ts, that took these
# from a form and kept them in the browser with `connected: true` seeded from
# placeholder values — so every calling screen rendered a working phone system
# on a deployment with no Twilio at all. It is deleted. Unset here means unset,
# and the screens say so.
#
# Account SID + Auth Token: Twilio Console home. BOTH must be set — a SID with
# no token is not a degraded connection, it is a 401 on every request.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=

# The number Yipyy sends from when NO FACILITY IS INVOLVED — status-page SMS, a
# platform notice. A facility's own messages go from its own number, out of
# public.communication_numbers. STATUS_SMS_FROM below is honoured as a fallback
# because it predates this variable.
TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=

# Twilio reaches this deployment at NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL (or NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_DOMAIN)
# — see the webhook URLs on the Integrations screen. It will not POST to
# http://localhost and requires HTTPS, so a local deployment SENDS perfectly
# well and RECEIVES nothing. That difference is on the screen rather than left
# to look like a broken number.

# ── Payments (Clover) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These identify YIPYY as a Clover app, not any facility. Each facility
# authorises that app against their OWN Clover merchant account via OAuth v2,
# and their tokens are stored per-facility in Supabase Vault
# (private.payment_credentials) — never here, and never in the browser.
#
# There is a client-side store, src/lib/clover-config-store.ts, that takes an
# App Secret from a form and writes it to window.localStorage. Nothing
# server-side reads it, and nothing should: a payment app secret in every
# admin's browser is the credential that lets someone charge cards.
#
# From your Clover developer dashboard. The sandbox and production apps have
# DIFFERENT ids and secrets, and pointing a sandbox app at api.clover.com fails
# with an error that looks like a bad secret.
#
# ── BOTH ESTATES RUN AT ONCE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A connection is served by the estate it was MADE against, which is stored on
# the row. So sandbox merchants keep working after you start connecting real
# ones, and there is always somewhere to test without touching real cards.
#
# Set the estate-scoped variables below for that. The unscoped CLOVER_APP_ID /
# CLOVER_APP_SECRET / CLOVER_REMOTE_APPLICATION_ID are still honoured, but ONLY
# for the estate CLOVER_ENVIRONMENT names — so production can never silently
# inherit sandbox credentials because somebody set one variable and not the
# other. A missing production secret means "not configured", which refuses.
CLOVER_APP_ID=
CLOVER_APP_SECRET=

# The estate-scoped form. Add these when you have a production app; keep the
# sandbox ones set at the same time.
CLOVER_SANDBOX_APP_ID=
CLOVER_SANDBOX_APP_SECRET=
CLOVER_SANDBOX_REMOTE_APPLICATION_ID=
CLOVER_PRODUCTION_APP_ID=
CLOVER_PRODUCTION_APP_SECRET=
CLOVER_PRODUCTION_REMOTE_APPLICATION_ID=

# The Remote Application ID — "{developerId}.{appId}", NOT the App ID. Card
# PRESENT payments are gated on it: every /connect/v1/* call answers 401
# "no Remote Application ID has been configured" until it exists.
#
# Generated in App Settings > App Type: pick the devices, tick REST Clients >
# Web, and answer YES to "is this an integration with an existing point of
# sale?". Saving reveals it. Sandbox and production apps each have their own.
CLOVER_REMOTE_APPLICATION_ID=

# Where a facility connecting TODAY is sent — not "the environment this
# deployment is in", which no longer exists as a concept. Existing connections
# keep using the estate they were made against.
#
# Each estate is a HOST PAIR, and mixing them (authorising on one, exchanging
# the code on the other) is the most common Clover integration failure, so it is
# one variable rather than two:
#   sandbox     sandbox.dev.clover.com   + apisandbox.dev.clover.com
#   production  www.clover.com           + api.clover.com
CLOVER_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox

# The X-Clover-Auth value from Your Apps > [app] > App Settings > Webhooks.
# Clover does NOT sign deliveries — this is a static shared secret repeated on
# every message — so /api/webhooks/clover rejects anything that does not carry
# it, and never acts on what a delivery says: it re-reads the named object from
# Clover's API before believing it.
#
# SET THIS LAST. The verification handshake POSTs {"verificationCode":"…"} with
# no auth header (Clover only starts sending one once the URL is verified), so
# that branch is open while this is unset and closes the moment it is set. The
# code is also written to payment_webhook_events.outcome, so you can read it out
# of the database rather than trawling deployment logs.
CLOVER_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# The password Vercel's scheduler sends back to your own app, proving a call to
# /api/cron/clover-sweep really came from the cron and not from the internet.
# That endpoint reads every connected merchant's payment history, so it REFUSES
# TO RUN without this rather than running unguarded:
#
#   unset      503 "CRON_SECRET is not set; the sweep will not run unguarded."
#              Vercel calls it on the schedule in vercel.json and gets a 503
#              every time. Nothing breaks, and nothing sweeps -- which is the
#              failure that hides, because a red job on a timer becomes
#              background noise and then the sweep has not run for a week.
#   mismatched 401. Should not happen: Vercel supplies both sides.
#
# Any long random string; nothing needs to remember it. Mint one with:
#   bun -e 'console.log(require("crypto").randomBytes(32).toString("base64url"))'
#
# Set it in the Vercel dashboard for PRODUCTION ONLY -- a preview deployment
# sweeping real Clover data is not something anybody asked for. Environment
# variables apply to NEW deployments only, so setting it does not change what is
# already running.
#
# The sweep also runs on demand from the "Reconcile now" button on the Yipyy Pay
# dashboard, which needs no secret and is the only way to exercise any of this
# locally, where no webhook can reach you.
CRON_SECRET=

# ── App ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
NEXT_PUBLIC_REALTIME_URL=

# ── Ops (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=
MONITORING_PROVIDER=
STATUS_SMS_FROM=

# ── Testing (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Password for the seeded @yipyy.dev development accounts, read by the e2e
# suite (tests/e2e/_auth.ts). Set this after rotating them with
# supabase/seed/rotate-dev-passwords.sql; unset, the suite falls back to the
# documented seed default.
E2E_PASSWORD=

# The card-payment spec (tests/e2e/clover-pay.spec.ts) needs a fixture no
# provisioning script can invent: a facility with a live Clover connection, the
# customer to pay as, and a staff member to create the booking as. Unset, that
# spec skips itself rather than failing with a puzzle — a Clover-connected
# merchant is a thing you set up by hand, once.
#
# It puts a REAL card through Clover's sandbox using their documented decline
# number, so it takes no money and leaves no ledger row. Do not point it at a
# production connection.
#
# CLOVER_E2E_BOOKING_REF IS RETIRED (2026-08-22) and nothing reads it. The spec
# used to name a booking somebody had made by hand and everybody hoped stayed
# unpaid; the first run after the WorkOS migration found it "Paid in full", and
# no booking at that facility had a balance at all. It now CREATES its own
# booking as staff and cancels it afterwards — which is why the staff email
# below is required for that spec and not only for the refund one.
CLOVER_E2E_CUSTOMER_EMAIL=

# The refund spec (tests/e2e/clover-refund.spec.ts) needs more, and SPENDS what
# it is given: an UNREFUNDED Clover payment on a booking, plus a staff member
# who holds `process_refund` at that facility. It gives real money back and
# leaves the booking fully refunded, so it cannot run twice against the same
# charge — hence its own booking ref, separate from the one above.
CLOVER_E2E_REFUND_BOOKING_REF=
CLOVER_E2E_STAFF_EMAIL=

# The terminal spec (tests/e2e/clover-terminal.spec.ts) needs a real Clover
# device associated with the connected merchant — a Dev Kit somebody physically
# set up. It only ASKS whether the device is awake, so it takes no money and is
# safe to run repeatedly; taking an actual card payment ends with a human
# tapping and cannot be automated.
CLOVER_E2E_TERMINAL_BOOKING_REF=
CLOVER_E2E_TERMINAL_SERIAL=

# The booking-detail spec (tests/e2e/booking-detail.spec.ts) checks that a
# facility booking page renders a client that lives in POSTGRES rather than in
# src/data/clients.ts. It reads nothing and writes nothing, so it is safe to run
# whenever.
#
# Point it at a client the FIXTURES DO NOT CONTAIN — anything created since the
# migration. Aimed at a fixture id it passes against the bug it exists to catch,
# because the fixture would satisfy the lookup. Fixture ids currently run to 53
# with gaps; check src/data/clients.ts before choosing.
E2E_POSTGRES_CLIENT_REF=
E2E_POSTGRES_BOOKING_REF=
E2E_POSTGRES_CLIENT_NAME=

# An OWNER at a facility that is NOT in src/data/facilities.ts — anything
# created since the migration. tests/e2e/facility-shell.spec.ts uses it to prove
# the sidebar names the facility from the DATABASE: the demo facility's legacy
# id is 11, which is the one id the fixture also contains, so against that
# account alone the old bug and the fix look identical.
#
# Unset, that assertion skips. The identity must exist in WORKOS, not merely in
# Postgres — the migration provisioned the seven @yipyy.dev accounts and nothing
# else, so the real-email owners currently cannot sign in at all.
E2E_NON_FIXTURE_OWNER=

# A client at a DIFFERENT facility, with payments of their own. The billing spec
# uses it to prove `payments_read` refuses across a tenant boundary — an empty
# result for a client who has no payments would prove nothing.
E2E_OTHER_CLIENT_REF=

# The apex this deployment answers on. Facility subdomains are <slug>.<this>.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_DOMAIN=

# ── Facility subdomains (spec 002 D2) ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every facility gets its own host — <slug>.yipyy.com — and provisioning
# attaches it to the Vercel project so a certificate is issued. DNS is already
# handled by a single wildcard CNAME at the registrar; these only control
# CERTIFICATE issuance. See src/lib/vercel/domains.ts for why there is no
# `*.yipyy.com` wildcard domain.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED STATE, not a broken one: a local dev server, a preview or
# a self-hosted instance creates facilities normally and reports that the
# subdomain was not attached. Only production needs these.
#
# A Vercel API token with access to the project (vercel.com/account/tokens).
# DOMAINS_* rather than VERCEL_* because Vercel injects its own VERCEL_PROJECT_ID
# and VERCEL_TEAM_ID and may refuse a custom variable under that prefix.
DOMAINS_API_TOKEN=
DOMAINS_PROJECT_ID=
# Omit on a personal-account project — Vercel rejects an empty teamId rather
# than ignoring it.
DOMAINS_TEAM_ID=
